    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `local-address 3.3.3.3` specifies the source address for BGP sessions\\n- In Cisco, we achieve the same by:\\n  1. First entering BGP configuration mode with `router bgp 100` (matching the group number)\\n  2. Then specifying the neighbor and using `update-source Loopback0` since 3.3.3.3 is configured on lo0 in the original config\\n- The neighbor IP (2.2.2.2) is taken from the neighbor statement in the original config', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `local-address 3.3.3.3` specifies the source address for BGP sessions\\n- In Cisco, we achieve the same by:\\n  1. First entering BGP configuration mode with `router bgp 100` (matching the group number)\\n  2. Then specifying the neighbor and using `update-source Loopback0` since 3.3.3.3 is configured on lo0 in the original config\\n- The neighbor IP (2.2.2.2) is taken from the neighbor statement in the original config', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
