    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco configuration requires first entering BGP configuration mode with the AS number (100 in this case), then specifying the neighbor and its AS number. The Juniper command combines these into a single set statement, while Cisco separates them into hierarchical configuration commands.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco configuration requires first entering BGP configuration mode with the AS number (100 in this case), then specifying the neighbor and its AS number. The Juniper command combines these into a single set statement, while Cisco separates them into hierarchical configuration commands.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [neighbor_ip] bfd-liveness-detection minimum-interval [interval]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 fall-over bfd\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 bfd interval 300 min_rx 300 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"fall-over bfd\" enables BFD for the BGP neighbor\\n2. The interval values are in milliseconds (300ms in this case)\\n3. Cisco requires specifying both transmit and receive intervals, plus a multiplier (default is 3)\\n4. The BGP AS number (100) must be specified in the \"router bgp\" context first', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 fall-over bfd', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 fall-over bfd\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 bfd interval 300 min_rx 300 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"fall-over bfd\" enables BFD for the BGP neighbor\\n2. The interval values are in milliseconds (300ms in this case)\\n3. Cisco requires specifying both transmit and receive intervals, plus a multiplier (default is 3)\\n4. The BGP AS number (100) must be specified in the \"router bgp\" context first', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 fall-over bfd'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 bfd interval 300 min_rx 300 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 fall-over bfd\\n neighbor 2.2.2.2 bfd interval 300 min_rx 300 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"fall-over bfd\" enables BFD for the BGP neighbor\\n2. The interval values are in milliseconds (300ms in this case)\\n3. Cisco requires specifying both transmit and receive intervals, plus a multiplier (default is 3)\\n4. The BGP AS number (100) must be specified in the \"router bgp\" context first', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2.2.2.2 bfd interval 300 min_rx 300 multiplier 3'}]]": 1,
    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command into Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\nFor the command:\\n```\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/0.0\\n```\\n\\nThe Cisco equivalent is:\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you need to first enter the interface configuration mode, then enable MPLS on that interface with the `mpls ip` command. The `.0` subinterface notation from Juniper isn't needed in this basic Cisco configuration as we're applying it to the main interface.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command into Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\nFor the command:\\n```\\nset protocols mpls interface ge-1/0/0.0\\n```\\n\\nThe Cisco equivalent is:\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you need to first enter the interface configuration mode, then enable MPLS on that interface with the `mpls ip` command. The `.0` subinterface notation from Juniper isn't needed in this basic Cisco configuration as we're applying it to the main interface.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ip'}]]": 1,
